-- JavaScript's strings: `JSString`, text as JavaScript has it, UTF-16,
-- beside a `String`, which is bytes (UTF-8 as a literal writes it, but
-- any byte may be in one).  A string is made one by `toJS`, and one
-- made a string by `fromJS`; there is nothing else to do with one.
--
-- It is for where a program meets JavaScript as it is:
--
-- - a field of an effect's operation of type `JSString` is given to its
--   provider, and taken from it, as a JavaScript string, through no
--   helper (a `String` field is bytes, which a provider gives to the
--   host by `$toJs` or `$toBytes`: lib/std/dom.provider.js says which);
--   a provider handing much text from one host API to another (what is
--   in a `<textarea>`, to another element) keeps it a `JSString`, made
--   a `String` only where the program reads it.  The library's own
--   effects (`std.dom`, `std.io`, ...) take and give `String`s, their
--   providers turning them into what the host has;
-- - an ES module (`--backend=esm`) gives and takes what it is given as
--   it is: a function for JavaScript to call takes and gives
--   `JSString`s, `greet : JSString -> JSString`, and is a `String`'s
--   inside, by `fromJS` and `toJS` (a `String` it gives is bytes, each
--   a code unit, which JavaScript must decode).
--
-- On every backend: in JavaScript (`esm`, `page`, `node`) a JavaScript
-- string; in C and the interpreter a string of UTF-8, valid, which is
-- all a `toJS` makes -- so `fromJS (toJS s)` is `R.toValid s` (lib/rune.hf)
-- on each.

-- | Text as JavaScript has it, a JavaScript string: made only by `toJS`
-- | (no value is written of it), and read only by `fromJS`.
data JSString

primitive strToJS : String -> JSString
primitive strFromJS : JSString -> String

-- | A string's text as JavaScript's: its UTF-8 read, what is not UTF-8
-- | in it U+FFFD (each longest start of a rune's bytes one, as
-- | `TextDecoder` reads them, a byte order mark kept).
toJS : String -> JSString
toJS = strToJS

-- | JavaScript's text as a string, UTF-8: a surrogate not in a pair
-- | U+FFFD (as `toWellFormed` makes it).  Back from `toJS`, a string
-- | is what it was where it was UTF-8.
-- | >>> List.map (\s -> fromJS (toJS s)) ["", "héllo 😀", "a" ^ fromByte 255 ^ "b", fromByte 237 ^ fromByte 160 ^ fromByte 128]
-- | ["", "héllo 😀", "a\u{FFFD}b", "\u{FFFD}\u{FFFD}\u{FFFD}"]
fromJS : JSString -> String
fromJS = strFromJS
